1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,170
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper, G2N2, presented at ICLR 2024,</font>

2
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:09,141
<font color="#FFFFFF">asks a deceptively simple question: can we design a
graph neural network whose expressive</font>

3
00:00:09,221 --> 00:00:11,556
<font color="#FFFFFF">power is guaranteed by construction,</font>

4
00:00:11,636 --> 00:00:13,836
<font color="#FFFFFF">rather than proved after the fact?</font>

5
00:00:13,916 --> 00:00:15,581
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors give a recipe.</font>

6
00:00:15,661 --> 00:00:22,758
<font color="#FFFFFF">They take a fragment of an algebraic matrix
language known to match the third-order Weisfeiler-Lehman test,</font>

7
00:00:22,838 --> 00:00:28,259
<font color="#FFFFFF">write it as a context-free grammar, prune the
grammar down to its essential rules,</font>

8
00:00:28,339 --> 00:00:33,357
<font color="#FFFFFF">and then translate those rules directly into the
layers of a neural network.</font>

9
00:00:33,437 --> 00:00:38,992
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result is a provably three-W-L graph neural
network that is both principled and,</font>

10
00:00:39,072 --> 00:00:42,950
<font color="#FFFFFF">in practice, faster and more accurate than its
competitors.</font>

11
00:00:43,330 --> 00:00:45,892
<font color="#FFFFFF">How expressive is a graph neural network?</font>

12
00:00:45,972 --> 00:00:50,725
<font color="#FFFFFF">For years the field has answered that with the
Weisfeiler-Lehman hierarchy,</font>

13
00:00:50,805 --> 00:00:55,881
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the gold standard has been to design a model
and then prove it matches, say,</font>

14
00:00:55,961 --> 00:00:57,234
<font color="#FFFFFF">the third-order test.</font>

15
00:00:57,314 --> 00:01:01,423
<font color="#FFFFFF">But that proof comes after the design, almost as an
afterthought.</font>

16
00:01:01,503 --> 00:01:07,416
<font color="#FFFFFF">What has been missing is a systematic way to go the
other direction: to start from a language</font>

17
00:01:07,496 --> 00:01:10,509
<font color="#FFFFFF">we already know is exactly as powerful as 3-W-L,</font>

18
00:01:10,589 --> 00:01:14,247
<font color="#FFFFFF">and mechanically build a network that inherits that power.</font>

19
00:01:14,327 --> 00:01:19,660
<font color="#FFFFFF">Without such a recipe, every expressive
architecture is a fresh, hand-crafted proof.</font>

20
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:25,848
<font color="#FFFFFF">The seed of the idea comes from a groundbreaking
observation: the 1-W-L and 3-W-L tests</font>

21
00:01:25,928 --> 00:01:30,722
<font color="#FFFFFF">can each be rewritten as a fragment of a matrix
language called MATLANG.</font>

22
00:01:30,802 --> 00:01:37,016
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two graphs look the same to 3-W-L if and only if
every sentence you can write in the fragment</font>

23
00:01:37,096 --> 00:01:39,724
<font color="#FFFFFF">ML-of-L-three gives them the same value.</font>

24
00:01:39,804 --> 00:01:43,852
<font color="#FFFFFF">That is a beautiful bridge between combinatorics
and algebra.</font>

25
00:01:43,932 --> 00:01:45,680
<font color="#FFFFFF">But a bridge is not a road.</font>

26
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:48,793
<font color="#FFFFFF">Turning one of these fragments into an actual,</font>

27
00:01:48,873 --> 00:01:52,177
<font color="#FFFFFF">trainable network had been done only case by case,</font>

28
00:01:52,257 --> 00:01:56,644
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the resulting models could not claim the full
3-W-L guarantee.</font>

29
00:01:56,724 --> 00:02:00,570
<font color="#FFFFFF">The motivation here is to pave that road once and for all.</font>

30
00:02:00,950 --> 00:02:04,640
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper makes four contributions.</font>

31
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:08,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

32
00:02:08,490 --> 00:02:12,180
<font color="#FFFFFF">a generic framework that turns any fragment of an
algebraic language into a graph neural</font>

33
00:02:12,260 --> 00:02:15,950
<font color="#FFFFFF">network through context-free grammars.</font>

34
00:02:16,030 --> 00:02:19,720
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, it runs that framework on the ML-of-L-three
fragment and out comes G2N2,</font>

35
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:23,490
<font color="#FFFFFF">a network that is provably 3-W-L.</font>

36
00:02:23,570 --> 00:02:27,260
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third, it validates the rule set experimentally,</font>

37
00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:31,030
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing that the grammar reduction keeps
expressiveness while trimming redundancy.</font>

38
00:02:31,110 --> 00:02:34,800
<font color="#FFFFFF">And fourth, across a broad battery of downstream tasks,</font>

39
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:38,570
<font color="#FFFFFF">G2N2 beats the existing 3-W-L networks, often while
running faster.</font>

40
00:02:38,950 --> 00:02:41,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here is the recipe in three moves.</font>

41
00:02:41,290 --> 00:02:47,059
<font color="#FFFFFF">Start from the operation set L-three: matrix
product, transpose, the all-ones vector,</font>

42
00:02:47,139 --> 00:02:49,467
<font color="#FFFFFF">diagonal, and element-wise product.</font>

43
00:02:49,547 --> 00:02:55,523
<font color="#FFFFFF">Write down an exhaustive grammar whose sentences
are exactly the fragment ML-of-L-three.</font>

44
00:02:55,603 --> 00:02:57,243
<font color="#FFFFFF">Then reduce that grammar,</font>

45
00:02:57,323 --> 00:03:03,368
<font color="#FFFFFF">stripping away redundant rules and variables until
only the essential productions remain,</font>

46
00:03:03,448 --> 00:03:06,809
<font color="#FFFFFF">while proving each step keeps the 3-W-L guarantee.</font>

47
00:03:06,889 --> 00:03:12,589
<font color="#FFFFFF">Now the magic: the surviving variables tell you
what the network's inputs should be,</font>

48
00:03:12,669 --> 00:03:16,099
<font color="#FFFFFF">and each surviving rule becomes a piece of a layer.</font>

49
00:03:16,179 --> 00:03:20,572
<font color="#FFFFFF">Concretely, a layer carries an edge memory C and a
node memory H;</font>

50
00:03:20,652 --> 00:03:24,425
<font color="#FFFFFF">learnable linear blocks combine slices of these tensors,</font>

51
00:03:24,505 --> 00:03:28,761
<font color="#FFFFFF">the reduced rules like M-times-M and M-Hadamard-M
are computed,</font>

52
00:03:28,841 --> 00:03:32,270
<font color="#FFFFFF">and two small MLPs stitch everything back together.</font>

53
00:03:32,350 --> 00:03:37,500
<font color="#FFFFFF">Stack these layers, add permutation-equivariant
readouts, and you have G2N2.</font>

54
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:41,284
<font color="#FFFFFF">The evaluation spans three very different arenas.</font>

55
00:03:41,364 --> 00:03:46,900
<font color="#FFFFFF">For regression, the QM9 dataset of one hundred
thirty thousand small molecules,</font>

56
00:03:46,980 --> 00:03:51,023
<font color="#FFFFFF">with twelve quantum-chemical targets, including R-squared,</font>

57
00:03:51,103 --> 00:03:52,943
<font color="#FFFFFF">the hardest one to predict.</font>

58
00:03:53,023 --> 00:03:56,213
<font color="#FFFFFF">For classification, the classic TUD benchmark,</font>

59
00:03:56,293 --> 00:04:02,043
<font color="#FFFFFF">six datasets ranging from molecules like MUTAG and
PTC to social graphs like IMDB.</font>

60
00:04:02,123 --> 00:04:04,247
<font color="#FFFFFF">And for a spectral stress test,</font>

61
00:04:04,327 --> 00:04:11,996
<font color="#FFFFFF">a node-regression task on nine-hundred-node graphs
that asks whether the model can act as a band-pass filter.</font>

62
00:04:12,076 --> 00:04:14,911
<font color="#FFFFFF">Together they probe accuracy, generality,</font>

63
00:04:14,991 --> 00:04:19,390
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a subtle spectral ability that trips up other
3-W-L models.</font>

64
00:04:19,770 --> 00:04:24,038
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline result is that G2N2 does not just
match the theory,</font>

65
00:04:24,118 --> 00:04:25,737
<font color="#FFFFFF">it dominates in practice.</font>

66
00:04:25,817 --> 00:04:28,387
<font color="#FFFFFF">On QM9, learning targets one at a time,</font>

67
00:04:28,467 --> 00:04:33,755
<font color="#FFFFFF">it posts the best error on every single target
while training faster than PPGN.</font>

68
00:04:33,835 --> 00:04:36,540
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the notoriously hard R-squared target,</font>

69
00:04:36,620 --> 00:04:39,598
<font color="#FFFFFF">its error drops to zero-point-three-four-two,</font>

70
00:04:39,678 --> 00:04:44,830
<font color="#FFFFFF">where PPGN sits at three-point-seven-eight, more
than a ten-fold improvement,</font>

71
00:04:44,910 --> 00:04:49,654
<font color="#FFFFFF">and when all twelve targets are learned at once the
gap widens further.</font>

72
00:04:49,734 --> 00:04:55,701
<font color="#FFFFFF">On graph classification it beats the second-best
network on five of the six TUD datasets.</font>

73
00:04:55,781 --> 00:05:00,593
<font color="#FFFFFF">And on the spectral test it cleanly learns
band-pass filters where PPGN,</font>

74
00:05:00,673 --> 00:05:04,330
<font color="#FFFFFF">starved of the memory it would need, essentially fails.</font>

75
00:05:04,710 --> 00:05:08,852
<font color="#FFFFFF">The most instructive experiment is the
grammar-reduction ablation.</font>

76
00:05:08,932 --> 00:05:12,562
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors compare the full grammar, an intermediate one,</font>

77
00:05:12,642 --> 00:05:16,848
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the reduced grammar r-G-of-L-three on the QM9
R-squared target.</font>

78
00:05:16,928 --> 00:05:19,279
<font color="#FFFFFF">Their errors are essentially the same,</font>

79
00:05:19,359 --> 00:05:24,844
<font color="#FFFFFF">which confirms that reduction throws away
redundancy without touching expressive power.</font>

80
00:05:24,924 --> 00:05:29,833
<font color="#FFFFFF">But when you push past the reduced grammar and
start deleting essential rules,</font>

81
00:05:29,913 --> 00:05:32,456
<font color="#FFFFFF">performance degrades in a measurable way.</font>

82
00:05:32,536 --> 00:05:38,725
<font color="#FFFFFF">That degradation is actually useful information: it
tells you how much each operation contributes,</font>

83
00:05:38,805 --> 00:05:44,610
<font color="#FFFFFF">so you can prune the model deliberately when a task
does not demand the full 3-W-L strength.</font>

84
00:05:44,990 --> 00:05:47,160
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

85
00:05:47,240 --> 00:05:48,932
<font color="#FFFFFF">On QM9's R-squared target,</font>

86
00:05:49,012 --> 00:05:53,704
<font color="#FFFFFF">error falls from three-point-seven-eight to
zero-point-three-four-two,</font>

87
00:05:53,784 --> 00:05:55,953
<font color="#FFFFFF">roughly an eleven-fold reduction,</font>

88
00:05:56,033 --> 00:06:02,361
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the model does it in ninety-eight seconds per
epoch versus PPGN's one hundred twenty-nine.</font>

89
00:06:02,441 --> 00:06:04,474
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the spectral band-pass task,</font>

90
00:06:04,554 --> 00:06:10,132
<font color="#FFFFFF">its R-squared score of zero-point-eight-two towers
over PPGN's zero-point-one-zero.</font>

91
00:06:10,212 --> 00:06:16,540
<font color="#FFFFFF">And on the TUD classification suite it lands better
than second place on five of six datasets,</font>

92
00:06:16,620 --> 00:06:19,880
<font color="#FFFFFF">including ninety-two-and-a-half percent on MUTAG.</font>

93
00:06:20,260 --> 00:06:22,994
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting message is a change of workflow.</font>

94
00:06:23,074 --> 00:06:28,047
<font color="#FFFFFF">Instead of designing a graph network and then
hoping to prove it is expressive,</font>

95
00:06:28,127 --> 00:06:32,524
<font color="#FFFFFF">you can start from a language whose expressive
power you already know,</font>

96
00:06:32,604 --> 00:06:37,256
<font color="#FFFFFF">reduce it to a clean grammar, and read the network
straight off the rules,</font>

97
00:06:37,336 --> 00:06:39,942
<font color="#FFFFFF">expressiveness guaranteed by construction.</font>

98
00:06:40,022 --> 00:06:45,571
<font color="#FFFFFF">G2N2 is the concrete payoff of that idea: a
provably 3-W-L model that is faster and more</font>

99
00:06:45,651 --> 00:06:47,553
<font color="#FFFFFF">accurate than its predecessors.</font>

100
00:06:47,633 --> 00:06:49,920
<font color="#FFFFFF">And because the framework is generic,</font>

101
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:55,292
<font color="#FFFFFF">the same grammatical route could turn other
algebraic fragments into other networks,</font>

102
00:06:55,372 --> 00:06:58,170
<font color="#FFFFFF">each carrying its expressive power by design.</font>
